+# Improve review — run 01M299PVMSE7Q53ZGPP67AHNMG
+
+- workflow: develop
+- branch integrated: this revisor pass (unmerged until approved)
+- status: succeeded (46.9 min, revisor pass — reason and cost in run detail)
+- generated: 2026-09-11 23:45+0000 by revisor `fabro_ask`
+
+---
+
+All evidence gathered — run events (stage conclusions, per-tool-call transcripts), the stage journal, and the workspace script files. Here are the recommendations, ordered by expected impact.
+
+**Run baseline for sizing** (from run events / conclusion): 46m53s wall, $2.52 total. Implementer alone: 37.4 min / $2.27 (90% of cost, 25.6 min of it tool time). Everything below attacks that concentration plus one correctness bug that actually fired.
+
+---
+
+**1. `evidence.nu` picked the wrong seed — feed it `current_seed_id` instead of letting it scan the tracker** *(correctness bug, fired this run)*
+- **What happened**: The capture header and "in-progress seed spec" section named **fabro-5082**, not this run's seed fabro-a3d8 (reviewer journal painpoint, 23:21; the evidence preview in the reviewer prompt confirms `seed=fabro-5082`). Root cause is in the workspace file `.fabro/workflows/develop/scripts/evidence.nu:171-172` — `def in-progress-seed []` runs `sd list --status in_progress --format json` and picks from that list; two seeds were in_progress concurrently, and it took the other one. The reviewer had to notice and disambiguate against the context keys; a less careful pass verifies against the wrong spec.
+- **Change**: Add `stdin_source="current_seed_id"` to the `evidence` node in `.fabro/workflows/develop/workflow.fabro` (the exact mechanism `closeout` already uses — proven in this run: closeout closed precisely fabro-a3d8) and make `in-progress-seed` prefer the stdin id, falling back to the scan only when stdin is empty.
+- **Expected effect**: Eliminates the mislabeled-capture failure class entirely; the reviewer never has to adjudicate a spec/tracker mismatch again.
+
+**2. Prime the cargo registry/target cache in the run sandbox** *(~9–11 min per Rust seed)*
+- **What happened**: The implementer's first ~9 minutes were pure cold-cache work: `cargo fetch` took ~3 min (tool call at seq 147, 22:37:14 → next call 22:40:19) and the first `cargo build -p fabro-llm -p fabro-workflow` ~5.5 min (seq 494, 22:48:29 → seq 500, 22:53:58). The container image `fabro-toolchain:noble` (run settings) ships no cargo cache. This is dead time on every develop run touching Rust.
+- **Change**: Bake a primed `$CARGO_HOME` registry + a cargo-chef-style `target` layer into `.fabro/Dockerfile.toolchain` (or mount a persistent cargo cache into the sandbox plan). Note: this touches an agent-reachable surface, so it's ADR-0019 capability-affecting and needs the explicit user-approval note — same category as open seed fabro-9bf4.
+- **Expected effect**: ~8–10 min less wall time per Rust seed (~20% of this run's total), and the tester gate's first compile goes warm too.
+
+**3. Quarantine the known-flaky `e2e_stall_watchdog_with_explicit_timeout_override` test** *(~8–9 min this run, plus gate-red bounce risk)*
+- **What happened**: Chasing that flake, the implementer ran the full fabro-workflow suite **four times** plus two single-test runs (tool calls at seq 540→546→552→558→564→570→576, spanning 22:57–23:07 ≈ 10 min, including two stash-verified clean-tree runs proving the flake pre-exists — its journal painpoint says it fails identically on clean trees, 2–9 timeouts per run). The tester gate got lucky this time; the implementer prompt itself cites a prior ~22-min gate-red bounce from exactly this class.
+- **Change**: In the repo's test config, mark that test flaky — `#[ignore = "flaky under parallel load, tracked in <seed>"]` or a nextest `retries = 1`/profile override for it — and file the seed the implementer's journal asked for.
+- **Expected effect**: Implementer verification drops to one suite run; removes a standing ~22-min tail risk on every gate pass over fabro-workflow.
+
+**4. Cap the loop-churn section of the evidence capture** *(kills the 95 KB blob detour)*
+- **What happened**: The evidence capture was **94,873 bytes** (evidence stage output_bytes), so it reached the reviewer as a blob-ref marker despite `preamble_inline_max_kb=16` and a 24 KB graph budget — forcing the reviewer's tool round-trip to page the blob (its 2.9% context-window usage shows the 1M window was never the constraint; the byte budget is). The bloat is the anomaly section's *full* diffs of changed loop files — the `.seeds/issues.jsonl` hunks alone embed multi-KB seed bodies (visible in the closeout diff).
+- **Change**: In `evidence.nu`, render loop-churn/anomaly files as numstat + the first ~30 changed lines each (full diffs stay for seed-work files only), keeping the "adjudicate every loop file" contract via the file list.
+- **Expected effect**: Typical capture lands under the reviewer's 16 KB inline ceiling — no blob detour, one shorter reviewer visit per cycle (this reviewer: 68.8s incl. blob read). Complements, not replaces, open seeds fabro-8d2c/fabro-9837.
+
+**5. One line in PROJECT_FACTS to stop the planner's wrong-path probing** *(~3 tool calls + LLM round trips per claim)*
+- **What happened**: The planner first probed `lib/foundation/fabro-llm/src/...` — a path that doesn't exist — because PROJECT_FACTS' "Primary code areas" names `lib/foundation/` while fabro-llm lives under `lib/components/` (events 45–59: two failed `rg` probes, a `fd: command not found`, then a symbol search that found it). Its own journal observed the mismatch but changed nothing, so the next planner repeats it.
+- **Change**: Add one line to `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/project-facts.md`: "Crates live under both `lib/components/` and `lib/foundation/` — resolve a seed's abbreviated paths by symbol search (`rg -ln <symbol>`), never by guessing the area prefix; `fd` is not installed."
+- **Expected effect**: ~3 fewer tool calls and 2–3 fewer model turns per fresh claim (~30–45 s and a few cents each run); removes a recurring probe pattern the journal already flagged twice.
